<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  QBX-233. THE TRACK DETAIL METER ASKS THE SOLO RULE ABOUT THE TRACK'S OWN
  ROOT, not about the master.

  THE DEFECT. ssolo::isLaneAudible is scoped to ONE summing container: it asks
  whether anything is soloed in THAT root, whether the lane is soloed, and
  whether the lane sits under a soloed ancestor of THAT root. Track Detail
  passed splacements::rootContainer(proj) – the master – unconditionally.
  That was harmless while the dock only ever showed a master track; QBX-231
  made it follow the active tab's root, so since then:
    - a solo anywhere in the master idled an arrangement track whose own tree
      has no solo at all;
    - a solo inside the arrangement was ignored, so a non-soloed sibling there
      kept metering;
    - underSoloedAncestor walked the master and never found the track's real
      folder ancestors.

  THE SEMANTICS WERE NOT A PRODUCT CHOICE. The ticket asked whether the dock
  should follow the holding root's solo state or "what the user hears through
  the master placement". The codebase answers it: of the twelve
  isLaneAudible call sites, ELEVEN pass the container that holds the lane –
  SStdMixer passes `this`, the arrangement's mixer view passes its own model,
  and smixerstrip's meter (the sibling of this one) passes its own mixer. This
  site was the only one passing the master, and it is the one that was not
  updated when QBX-231 landed. An asset fragment settles the alternative
  independently: its tracks may have NO placement in the master, or several, so
  "what you hear through the placement" has no answer there while this one
  always does.

  THE SEAM. The meter only runs while LIVE and VISIBLE, so a headless case
  cannot watch it. describeTrackDetailDock's `audible=` field reports
  STrackDetailPanel::meterAudible(), which is the function onMeterTick itself
  calls – not a second copy of the rule, which would let this case pass over a
  meter that stayed wrong.

  WHY TWO CLAIMS IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS. (a) expects audible=1 where the old
  code gave 0, and (b) expects audible=0 where it gave 1. A sabotage cannot
  satisfy both by reporting a constant, and neither claim alone would notice a
  rule that had simply been inverted.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="track_detail_meter_solo_scope">
  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackIndex="0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>
    <add-sample trackIndex="1" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>

    <create-arrangement name="Drums"/>
    <add-track arrangement="Drums" index="-1"/>
    <add-track arrangement="Drums" index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackPath="Drums:0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>
    <add-sample trackPath="Drums:1" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>

    <!-- The FIRST read ATTACHES the dock: a scripted run never reaches the
         attach sites, so it has to come before the gestures it must follow. -->
    <assert-track-detail-dock contains="|rows="/>
    <open-arrangement-tab name="Drums"/>
    <activate-tab name="Drums"/>

    <!-- TWO DIFFERENT ROOTING RULES ARE IN PLAY HERE, and mixing them up is
         what a first draft of this case did. `set-track-solo` reads its path
         with parseInto, so a BARE path is MASTER-rooted whatever tab is
         active, and an arrangement lane must be named "Drums:1" explicitly.
         `select-track` is a view GESTURE and follows the ACTIVE TAB, so a bare
         "0" there means Drums:0 while the Drums tab is active. Both spellings
         below are deliberate. -->

    <!-- ===== (a) A MASTER SOLO DOES NOT IDLE AN ARRANGEMENT TRACK =====
         Master lane 0 is soloed; the dock then shows an arrangement track
         whose own tree has no solo at all, so it is audible there. The old
         master-scoped read answered "something is soloed and this lane is not
         it" and idled the meter. -->
    <set-track-solo trackPath="0" solo="1"/>
    <select-track trackPath="0"/>
    <assert-track-detail-dock contains="|path=Drums:0"/>
    <assert-track-detail-dock contains="|audible=1"/>

    <!-- ===== (b) A SOLO INSIDE THE ARRANGEMENT IS HONOURED =============
         The master solo is cleared and Drums:1 is soloed instead, with the
         dock still on Drums:0. The master now holds no solo at all, so the old
         read answered "nothing is soloed, everything is audible" and reported
         a lit meter for a track its own root silences. -->
    <set-track-solo trackPath="0" solo="0"/>
    <set-track-solo trackPath="Drums:1" solo="1"/>
    <assert-track-detail-dock contains="|path=Drums:0"/>
    <assert-track-detail-dock contains="|audible=0"/>

    <!-- ...and the soloed sibling itself IS audible, so (b) cannot be
         satisfied by a rule that simply darkens the whole arrangement. -->
    <select-track trackPath="1"/>
    <assert-track-detail-dock contains="|path=Drums:1"/>
    <assert-track-detail-dock contains="|audible=1"/>
  </actions>
</SActionScript>
